\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five facts on Slovak cbank governor Sramko\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2008\par 11:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Dec 31 (Reuters) - Ivan Sramko, governor of the National Bank of Slovakia (NBS), will take a seat on the policy-making Governing Council of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) after Slovakia becomes the 16th member of the euro zone on Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 Following are five facts about Ivan Sramko:\par \par \uc2 -- Sramko was born on Sept. 3, 1957 in Bratislava and graduated from the Bratislava-based Economic University in 1980. He is married and has three children.\par \par \uc2 -- He became NBS governor on Jan. 1, 2005, after having served as the bank's deputy governor since Jan. 11, 2002.\par \par \uc2 -- Sramko joined the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from his post as a board member of Tatra Banka, Slovakia's third largest bank and a unit of Austria's Raiffeisen Bank. He was the CEO of small local bank Istrobanka before.\par \par \uc2 -- Under Sramko's leadership, the NBS repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown. It also propped up the crown in 2006 when it weakened due to investors fearing the new leftist government would dismantle market-friendly reforms and abandon the 2009 euro adoption target.\par \par \uc2 -- Sramko oversaw Slovakia's entry to the exchange rate mechanism ERM-2 in 2005 and two revaluations of the crown's parity rate within the pre-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grid. Before the unit was irrevocably fixed to the euro in July 2009, Sramko said the exchange rate had a minor impact on inflation and cautioned about the negative effect of too strong a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the economy, which the market viewed as a sign he was against an overly strong conversion rate for the euro switchover.\par \par \uc2 In the end, Prime Minister Robert Fico had advocated as strong a conversion rate as possible, and the fixing rate of 30.1260 crowns per euro was the unit's strongest level ever. (Reporting by Peter Laca; Editing by Michael Winfrey/Tony Austin)\par \par \uc2 SLOVAKIA-EURO/SRAMKO (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|CZS|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081231e4cv000fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
