\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on Oct 17   \b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2007\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Oct 17 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT MEETING   \par \par \uc2 Government will hold its regular weekly meeting, 0800 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 SEPT JOBLESS DATA   \par \par \uc2 Labour Office will release September jobless rate data, 0900 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 NO BIG NEED FOR RATE HIKE-SLOVAK CBANKER   \par \par \uc2 Slovak EU-norm inflation has picked up more than expected due to food price growth, but there is no big need to tighten monetary conditions, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) board member Ludovit Odor said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL16552908] [RTRS-PLCY-CEN--SK]   \par \par \uc2 SEPT CPI OFF RECORD LOW, BUT EURO ON TRACK   \par \par \uc2 Rising food prices lifted Slovak EU-norm inflation from record lows in September, but analysts said interest rates would stay on hold as the country appeared on track to meet a key euro entry test.   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL16530233] [RTRS-MCE-ECI-SK]   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL16891453] [RTRS-MCE-ECI-SK]   \par \par \uc2 C.BANK SAYS SEPT INFLATION ABOVE ITS FCAST   \par \par \uc2 Slovak EU-norm inflation, running at 1.7 percent year-on-year, was above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (NBS) forecast in September mainly due to a sharper increase in food prices, the NBS said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nPRG000544] [RTRS-MCE-ECI-CEN-SK]   \par \par \uc2 ENEL\uc2  MAY RETHINK SLOVAK INVESTMENT IF RULES CHANGE   \par \par \uc2 Italy's Enel could rethink its plans to invest $4.64 billion in Slovakia if the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the free energy market, Enel's \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL16265500] [RTRS-UTIL-ELEU-ELG-SK]   \par \par \uc2 CBANK DRAINS SKK 156 BLN IN REPO TENDER   \par \par \uc2 The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS) accepted all bids in a regular two-week repo tender on Tuesday, draining 156.046 billion crowns ($6.59 billion) from the market, NBS data showed.   \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL16362482] [RTRS-CEN-MMT-INT-SK]   \par \par \uc2 PRESS DIGEST   \par \par \uc2 ------------   \par \par \uc2 PENSION SYSTEM   \par \par \uc2 Slovak ruling coalition parties have agreed on changes to the pension system. Parliament is expected to debate the draft later his month.   \par \par \uc2 Sme, page 2   \par \par \uc2 FICO MEETS GYURSCANY   \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Robert Fico is expected to meet his Hungary's counterpart Ferenc Gyurscany in Lisbon later this week, seeking ways to calm political tensions between Slovakia and Hungary.   \par \par \uc2 Sme, page 2   \par \par \uc2 SONY LAUNCHES PRODUCTION   \par \par \uc2 Sony\uc2  starts mass production at its 73 million-euro ($103.7 million) plant near the western Slovak town of Nitra.   \par \par \uc2 Sme, page 6   \par \par \uc2 HIGHWAY CONSTRUCTION   \par \par \uc2 The government is expected to debate the transport ministry's plan to build 151 kilometers of highways through public-private partnerships. The finance ministry's analysis advises to cut the planned volume by 30 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Sme, page 9   \par \par \uc2 PM FICO ILL   \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Robert Fico was forced to scrap his program for an illness on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Pravda, page 4   \par \par \uc2 For news on upcoming events in the United States and other Group of Seven countries, see <G7TODAY>. For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click [SK/DIARY], and a calendar of east European economic indicators, see [CONV/DIARY].   \par \par \uc2 News editor of the day: Peter Laca on +421 2