\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Calm Trading Seen Ahead Of Easter   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2007\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1100/1000 Hungary 12-Month Treasury Bond Tender \par           N/A Vs 7.53% \par  \par 1430/1330 Hungary Treasury Schedule \par           N/A Vs HUF110B \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8377-84  3.8449-77  -0.19  3.8429  3.8384   +0.14 \par EUR/HUF 245.53-83  245.40-70  +0.05  245.53  245.80   -2.35 \par EUR/CZK 27.894-24  27.896-51  -0.01  27.920  27.924   +1.47 \par EUR/SKK 33.315-65  33.377-26  -0.19  33.335  33.365   -3.28 \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: A local dealer expects appreciation of the zloty in the medium-term, on "strong economic fundamentals and a steady inflow of new investors." Light volumes are expected ahead of the Easter break, with the Polish unit remaining rangebound, the dealer added.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Before returning close to levels seen Tuesday, the zloty weakened against the euro early Wednesday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at PLN3.8486, compared with PLN3.8460 late Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: The forint is expected to remain rangebound throughout the rest of the week, said a trader. Although domestic trade deficit figures Friday may provide some impetus, trading is expected to remain lackluster ahead of the Easter holidays.   \par \par \uc2 In line with the Polish zloty, the forint ended the day flat, after depreciating in reaction to Slovak developments in early trade. Late Wednesday, the euro was trading unchanged at HUF246.00.   \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: The euro is likely to gain against the koruna in the medium-term, said a Citibank economist, "largely on rising profit repatriation and a slowdown in direct investment inflows." With the domestic calendar empty for the remainder of the week, and Polish and Slovak markets closed Friday for the Easter weekend, the market is expected to remain rangebound, a trader said, with the euro trading at levels between CZK27.90 and CZK28.00.   \par \par \uc2 The koruna was little changed against the euro Wednesday, as the weakening of the Slovak koruna offset favorable foreign trade data, which came in above the general market consensus. Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at CZK27.94, compared with CZK27.97 late Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: Traders expect the bond market to remain uneventful as the Easter holiday draws closer.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish treasury bond prices were down Wednesday, as investors reduced their positions ahead of an expected inflation rise followed by expected rate hikes, traders said. "Until very recently some people still had doubts about higher rates," said a trader. "Right now we have only zealous believers." Late Wednesday, the average yield on the two-year paper was steady at 4.62%, while the five-year yield was unchanged at 5.00% and the 10-year yield was at 5.22%, up from 5.21%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: The focus will be on the 12-month treasury bond auction Thursday, said a trader, although overall the market is expected to remain quiet.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bond prices rose Wednesday, with yields one to two basis points lower across the curve, in very thin trade, a trader said. Late Wednesday, the three-year paper was yielding 7.27%, the five-year was yielding 7.04%, and the 10-year was yielding 6.71%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: The market is expected to remain calm and track moves in German bunds, largely due to a lack of any major economic news. Bond prices will also follow moves on regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, partly driven by the volatility of the Slovak koruna, traders said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's trading was calm. The benchmark 10-year Czech bond yielded an average of 3.88% late in the day, unchanged from late Tuesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                     3531.50  gain  10.21   up 0.3% \par BUX                      23871.15  gain 215.98   up 0.9% \par PX-D                      1745.20  gain  21.40   up 1.2% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15  3260.87   gain  20.34   up 0.6% \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: A local trader said the mood is supportive for a break of the February 3550 peak Thursday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WIG20 ended up 0.3% at 3531.50 as the index again failed to sustain a break above this technical resistance as profit-taking set in. Blue-chip moves were dominated by commodity issues moving in opposite directions. KGHM ended the day 2.8% higher as the bull run of copper continued, while PKN Orlen declined 2% as crude oil fell on an easing of geopolitical tensions. Elsewhere, TPSA remained weak, ending 0.9% lower on the day, while technology and media issues outperformed, especially Prkoom, which closed 4.1% higher, and TVN, which ended up 1.4% despite soft March viewership numbers. Retailers were hot on booming first-quarter sales numbers, including CCC, finishing 6.6% higher, and LPP, which ended up 18.9%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: A trader expects the BUX returning some gains in coming days, after the market hit a local peak for the time being.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX index ended up 0.9% at 23871.15 in robust trade, moving in tandem with core markets. Blue chips ended the day higher, with the exception of  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which remained flat. OTP finished 1.0% higher, and MOL was up 0.3% despite falling oil prices, while drug stocks Richter and Egis soared 2.9% and 0.34% higher, respectively. "Drug shares are undergoing a price correction after the heavy selling pressure seen recently, but I wouldn't call this a change of trend," a local trader said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Traders said as the current price of Telefonica O2 CR, or T-O2 CR, is still below the stock's new CZK750 target price, its market price may continue to rise.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PX index ended the day up 1.2% at 1745.2, led by T-O2 CR, which rallied 3.8% on a target price increase by Lehman Brothers. The stock ended at CZK579.50. The bullish trade in the stock was also fueled by solid dividend expectations, a trader said. The overall positive mood in local blue chips also helped CEZ, which ended 1.5% higher, and Zentiva, which finished up 0.3%. Only  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Orco bucked the trend, closing down 0.3% and 1.1%, respectively, largely on profit-taking after recent gains. [ 04-05-07 0057ET ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland has indicated it is prepared to lift its veto on talks on a new European Union-Russia partnership agreement, stalled over Moscow's ban on Polish meat and plant products, officials said Thursday. Russia has barred meat imports from Poland since late 2005, citing health concerns. The unresolved dispute led Warsaw to veto the negotiations on a deal that aims to forge closer energy ties between Moscow and the 27-nation bloc.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's health minister resigned Wednesday, in a letter addressed to the prime minister. The premier's office in a statement confirmed Lajos Molnar's resignation as of April 6 and said until a new minister was confirmed, Agnes Horvath, health state secretary, would be leading the ministry.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: President Traian Basescu signed Wednesday a decree appointing 12 new ministers, a day after parliament gave a vote of confidence to Prime Minister Calin Popescu Tariceanu. Basescu and Tariceanu have been at odds with each other for more than a year, and Tariceanu expelled from his new Cabinet his main coalition ally, which was close to Basescu.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania should boost its exports to counter a high current account deficit, which may worsen in coming years due to lower foreign direct investment, the Romanian Center for Economic Policies, or CEROPE, said in a report Wednesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romanian and Turkish state-run power grid operators  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transelectrica\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TEL.R0) and Teias are looking for a private partner to cover 25% of the costs entailed by a feasibility study of their joint underwater power cable project aiming to link the two countries' energy grids, news agency Mediafax reported Wednesday, citing  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transelectrica\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Lauren Parr, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9446; lauren.parr@dowjones.com [ 04-05-07 0058ET ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070405e3450000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
