\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Risk appetite cautiously returns   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sujata Rao   \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2007\par 12:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 4 (Reuters) - Investors' appetite for risk appeared to be recovering on Wednesday as emerging equity and bond markets bounced to record highs but \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned in a mixed picture with central Europe underperforming.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  knocked the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  crown down one percent to a week-low on Wednesday, pressuring central Europe but the Turkish lira hit an 11-month high against the dollar while the South African rand traded at a five-week high.   \par \par \uc2 Emerging equity markets jumped over one percent <.MSCIEF> to a record high, surpassing levels hit just before a Feb 27 fall in China helped trigger a two-week global selloff. Emerging sovereign bond spreads over U.S. Treasuries hit record tight levels late on Tuesday as cash poured into the high-risk sector.   \par \par \uc2 "Risk appetite is cautiously back," said Koon Chow, emerging markets strategist at  \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in London.   \par \par \uc2 Chow said high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Turkish lira, the Phillipine peso and the Brazilian real are benefiting most, with investors still nervous about global backdrop.   \par \par \uc2 "People are adding long EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions only where they can get compensated for risk by the yield. It's mainly global risks -- a combination of oil prices with the ongoing concerns about U.S. equity markets," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said volumes were thinner than usual as the long Easter break approaches and there was some reluctance to push \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  much higher in the meantime.   \par \par \uc2 Recent U.S. economic data has painted a mixed picture with no clarity on when the Federal Reserve could start cutting interest rates and investors are hoping to gauge the health of the economy from Friday's non-farm payrolls data.   \par \par \uc2 But some easing of tensions with Iran over the release of 15 captive British sailors and general expectations for a U.S. rate cut have helped re-ignite carry trades, in which investors sell low-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like yen to buy high-yield assets overseas.   \par \par \uc2 In some countries such as Poland and Mexico, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also receive support from monetary tightening, Chow said, noting local stories within many emerging markets have also improved.   \par \par \uc2 SLOVAKIA \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In Slovakia, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to experience bullish appreciation momentum, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to check the crown which rose to a two-week high around 33.100 per euro <EURSKK=> pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down one percent.   \par \par \uc2 The bank has repeatedly said it will not tolerate rampant crown appreciation, quashing investors who piled in after the crown parity to the euro was revalued 8.5 percent last month.   \par \par \uc2 The Slovak crown fall hit neighbouring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the forint is near two-week high against the euro and the zloty close to 10-week highs hit on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "Local (central Europe) markets were spoiled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Apart from that, everything looks tip-top as far as risk sentiment is concerned," said  \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  CA-IB economist Piotr Chwiejczak. "We also saw yen weakening over the last couple of days, which shows that risk appetite is increasing," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Another outperformer was the Israeli shekel which, despite the worries on Iran, inched higher to the strongest level since end-May 2001 against the dollar <ILS=>, thanks to equities which have powered higher after a series of rate cuts.   \par \par \uc2 "The prime conditions are in place for a strong shekel -- an equity rebound and a weak dollar," said Lucy Bethell, strategist at the  \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland.\par \par \uc2 The rand eased 0.20 percent to 7.17 per dollar, failing to pierce resistance around 7.15 -- a five-week high <ZAR=>. The lira hit an 11-month high of 1.3630 before paring gains <TRY>. Turk bonds and stocks have benefited from data showing March inflation below forecast, with equities up one percent <.XU100>.   \par \par \uc2 -additional reporting by Agnieszka Flak in London [20070404 102120 GMT]    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                 LATEST    PREV   LOCAL    LOCAL  \par                                 CLOSE  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                        PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par                                                 IN 2007\par  EUROPE/MIDDLE EAST/AFRICA\par  Czech Rep.   <EURCZK=>   27.93     27.97   +0.14    -1.60\par  Poland       <EURPLN=>    3.85      3.84   -0.28    -0.66\par  Hungary      <EURHUF=>  245.97    245.64   -0.13    +2.11\par  Slovakia     <EURSKK=>   33.48     33.12   -1.08    +2.81\par  Romania      <EURRON=>    3.33      3.33   -0.04    +1.30\par  Ukraine      <UAH=>       5.06      5.06   -0.06    -0.20\par  South Africa <ZAR=>       7.17      7.16   -0.17    -2.54\par  Kenya        <KES=>      69.10     68.45   -0.94    +0.65\par  Israel     