\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Region Awaits Retail Sales Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 06:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0800 Hungary Jan Retail Sales \par           N/A Vs +3.3% Y-Y \par  \par 1000/0900 Poland Feb Retail Sales \par           Seen +16.3% Y-Y Vs +16.5% Y-Y \par  \par 1000/0900 Poland Feb Unemployment \par           Preliminary estimate 15.0% Vs 15.1% \par  \par 1400/1300 Poland Jan Net Consumer Price Inflation \par           Seen +1.6% Y-Y Vs +1.6% Y-Y \par  \par 1400/1300 Poland Feb Net Consumer Price Inflation \par           Seen +1.6% Y-Y \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8661-11  3.8679-29  -0.05  3.8700  3.8690   +0.88 \par EUR/HUF 245.70-00  245.96-26  -0.11  245.78  245.87   -2.28 \par EUR/CZK 27.925-40  27.953-83  -0.10  27.925  27.939   +1.58 \par EUR/SKK 33.345-85  33.350-90  -0.01  33.350  33.380   -3.19 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: A correction may be in store for the zloty, says a local trader, after the market overreacted to the comments made by the U.S. Federal Reserve Wednesday. The possibility of a hike in U.S. interest rates remains, he adds. The focus Friday will be on February retail sales and net consumer price inflation data. Looking further ahead, the market will take direction from the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate-setting meeting, which ends March 28.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty firmed in line with the Hungarian forint Thursday on positive emerging markets sentiment, said a trader, after the U.S. Fed released what was interpreted to be a dovish accompanying statement.  \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the euro was trading at PLN3.8648 compared with PLN3.8733 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: In focus Friday will be domestic January retail sales data. Looking to next week, the market will await Monday's interest rate decision. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is largely expected to leave rates unchanged. A dealer expects the succeeding comments to indicate monetary easing in coming months, pending inflation developments.  \par \par \uc2 The forint firmed against the euro Thursday, on positive investor sentiment within the fixed income market, a trader said. Late Thursday, the euro was trading at HUF245.36, compared with HUF247.30 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: Given the lack of domestic impetus, the market will take its direction from the Slovak National Bank policy meeting March 27, says a trader. "The region will be dominated by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings next week," the trader adds, with the Slovak meeting of key importance for the Czech unit. With the increasing expectation of an interest rate cut and further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market a perpetual risk, the euro is expected to reach support at CZK28.15, the trader adds.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna was relatively unchanged Thursday amid calm trading, a local trader said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took its lead from the Slovak unit, which remained stable after its recent bout of volatility. Late Thursday, the euro was trading at CZK27.93, compared with CZK27.97 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: On the domestic front, the Polish market will focus on net consumer price inflation data and February retail sales figures Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish treasury bonds were largely quiet Thursday, with the long end of the curve weaker as foreign demand subsided. More activity was seen on forward rate agreements on the Warsaw Interbank Offer Rate, which rose along the curve as the market discounted a near-term rate hike, a local analyst said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the average yield on the two-year paper was unchanged from late Wednesday at 4.55%, while the five-year yield was at 4.95%, from 4.96%, and the 10-year yield was at 5.20%, from 5.15%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: The Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to the leave key rate steady Monday, but overnight money market rates are high and liquidity is tight as the market prepares for the possible but unlikely event of a rate cut. A local trader expects fixed income investor sentiment to remain positive as a result of continued fiscal progress.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bonds firmed on U.S. buying late Thursday, a bond trader said. Interest was boosted by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength and the dovish interpretation of the U.S. Fed's post-meeting statement. Hungarian bond yields fell 10 basis points on the short end and five basis points on the long end of the curve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the three-year paper was yielding 7.27%, the five-year was yielding 7.07% and the 10-year was yielding 6.60%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: Yields of local bonds may move higher Friday, following the similar moves the German bund market. "I'd say yields could rise a little, by about two basis points," said Petr Kaspar, a trader at Ceska Sporitelna. Yields are also likely to be pushed higher by the easing volatility on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, Kaspar said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday, yields on long-dated bonds rose slightly on the day, shadowing similar moves in bonds in core markets. The yield on the benchmark 10-year Czech paper rose two basis point on the day to 3.79%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                     3470.55  gain  68.40   up 2.0% \par BUX                      23341.85  gain 279.68   up 1.2% \par PX-D                      1691.00  gain   9.6    up 0.6% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15  3169.27   gain  50.26   up 1.6% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The outlook remains uncertain for the WIG20 index but near-term falls seem unlikely, says a local broker.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index ended up 2.0% at 3470.55 as massive buying lifted blue chips after the U.S. Fed's dovish comments late Wednesday. High volumes noted on liquid blue chips suggested foreign buyers' activity, a broker said. Ending in positive territory were Agora, which ended 4.4% higher; BRE, which closed 4.1% higher; KGHM, closing up 3.2% and PKN Orlen, which closed 3.3% higher. Among other gainers were TPSA, which finished up 2.6% and TVN, which closed 2.4% higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Profit-taking detected in OTP, MOL and Magyar Telekom may indicate a fall in coming days, a Raiffeisen Bank trader says.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX index ended 1.2% higher at 23341.85 in heavy trade. "The BUX closed up but that level was its daily low. That is significant as it signals that the market is losing steam after a five-day rally," the trader said. OTP Bank ended 1.3% higher, MOL ended up 1.5% and Magyar Telekom closed the day up 0.4%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Traders say Erste may face some profit-taking Friday or next week. "All the regional markets may undergo a little correction in coming days," a trader adds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PX index ended up 0.6% at 1691.0 Thursday led by Erste Bank, which closed 2.5% higher on the overall positive sentiment over banking stocks. Zentiva, the most actively traded stock, closed 1% lower, following gains earlier in the day. "Some investors seized an opportunity to take profits after Zentiva's week-long rally," the trader said. Among other stocks, Telefonica O2 CR ended 0.7% lower and Komercni Banka ended down 0.3% on steady profit-taking after recent gains. CEZ closed 0.3% higher. [ 03-23-07 0156ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Marek Mazur, chief executive of Poland's national carrier LOT Polish Airlines SA, has been fired from his post. Mazur, whose term was just 38 days long, told Polish news TV channel TVN24 he didn't know why the supervisory board removed him from the post.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Netherlands-based New World Resources is planning an initial public offering in the autumn of this year, the company's lead investor, Zdenek Bakala, said Thursday. The company is the holding that owns OKD AS, the Czech Republic's largest black-coal mining company by production volume.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: France Telecom (FTE.FR) said Thursday its Orange Slovensko subsidiary plans to spend EUR30 million on setting up an optic fiber network in Slovakia. The French company said the network will be the most dense national network of optical household connections in the country. The move fits in with France Telecom's goal of deploying a fixed telecommunication network based on FTTH (Fiber To The Home) technology in Slovakia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: German architectural software developer Nemetschek AG (NEM.XE) said Thursday that it has a 90.6% stake in Hungarian peer Graphisoft SE (GRAPHI.BU) following the completion of the public offer submitted to the Graphisoft shareholders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Russian President Vladimir Putin and Hungarian Prime Minister Ferenc Gyurcsany said Thursday that trade between their nations in on the rise and welcomed a sharp increase in Hungarian exports to Russia. Hosting Gyurcsany at his residence outside Moscow, Putin said Hungarian exports to Russia reached $2 billion last year, a figure the Kremlin says was an increase of two-thirds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The Nabucco pipeline project aiming to supply natural gas from the Caspian Sea to Central Europe started out as an economic endeavor, but has ended up as a political dispute, news agency Mediafax reported Thursday, citing Florin Muntean, general manager of Romanian state-run Transgaz, part of the Nabucco consortium.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Nokia Corp. (NOK) is to open a cell phone plant and a research center near the Transylvanian city of Cluj, Romanian officials said Thursday. The Finland-based company is expected to invest EUR200 million in the project to build a technology center in the village of Jucu that will employ 15,000 people, said Cluj prefect Marius Nicoara.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SERBIA: Serbia's largest political party, the ultranationalist Radicals, on Thursday demanded early general elections after moderate groups failed to form a Cabinet two months after January elections. The hardliners that once ruled alongside Slobodan Milosevic and now hope to return to power, blasted the pro-Western and moderately conservative parties that have been deadlocked in talks on sharing power in a coalition government.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Lauren Parr, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9446; lauren.parr@dowjones.com [ 03-23-07 0157ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070323e33n0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
