\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Region Weighs Fed's Softer Tone  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1200/1100 Hungary 12-Month Treasury Bond Tender \par 1530/1430 Hungary Treasury Schedule \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8610-50  3.8620-60  -0.03  3.8685  3.8614   +0.74 \par EUR/HUF 245.57-87  245.62-92  -0.02  245.74  245.85   -2.33 \par EUR/CZK 27.900-30  27.920-80  -0.07  27.925  27.925   +1.49 \par EUR/SKK 33.330-70  33.340-80  -0.03  33.360  33.365   -3.24 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: A local dealer expects the relatively dovish statement from the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting "could have a positive impact on emerging markets." On the domestic front, the market will eye February retail sales data Friday, with growth expected to stay red-hot at 16.3% on the year, compared with 16.5% in January, according to a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 11 analysts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The zloty weakened Wednesday in range-bound trade, as investors remained on the sidelines ahead of the FOMC meeting which is set to end after the market closes.  \par \par \uc2 Late in the day, the euro was trading at PLN3.8733, compared with PLN3.8730 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: The forint is expected to weaken in the near term, says a trader, after the finance ministry's denial earlier this week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band is to be abandoned in the foreseeable future. Further ahead, the market will await the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting meeting Monday, when most analysts expect rates to be left on hold. However, the recent strength of the forint has given a boost to expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might cut rates, says a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The forint weakened against the euro Wednesday, but kept close to the strong edge of its trading band, a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the euro was trading at HUF247.30, compared with HUF246.50 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: The koruna is expected to depreciate further in coming days, tracking the negative trend of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  koruna after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday. A local dealer sets a short-term target of CZK28.15 against the euro. With the domestic calendar empty for the remainder of the week, investors will follow core market developments, taking direction from the FOMC post-meeting statement, a trader says.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna weakened in calm trade Wednesday, in line with the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the euro rebounded from its losses earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the euro was quoted at CZK27.97, compared with CZK27.79 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: Thursday morning may bring some commotion after the FOMC interest rate decision and subsequent comments, a local trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish treasury bonds were little changed in sluggish trade Wednesday, said traders. A successful five-year treasury bond auction encouraged prices to climb slightly, but overall there was little fluctuation in the market, said a dealer.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the average yield on the two-year paper was at 4.51%, from 4.50% late Tuesday, while the five-year yield was at 5.00%, from 4.99%, and the 10-year yield was at 5.20%, from 5.19%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: A trader views a 50 basis point interest rate cut Monday as feasible, given the recent strength of the forint. Short maturities may add further gains Thursday, while the longer paper is likely to remain largely unchanged, he added. The focus Thursday will be on the 12-month treasury bond auction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds ended mixed Wednesday, with yields on the shorter end of the curve declining around 5 basis points amid intensifying expectations for an interest rate cut Monday, a local trader said. "The strengthening of short maturities is a sign that more and more investors expect a rate cut," he noted. Yields on longer maturities rose 3-6 basis points in tandem with the weakening of the forint.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the three-year paper was yielding 7.40%, the five-year was yielding 7.20%, and the 10-year was yielding 6.75%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: Traders will follow developments of bonds on core markets Thursday, namely the German bunds market, as no domestic data is scheduled, a local trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday, yields on long-dated bonds rose slightly on the day, shadowing similar moves in bonds in core markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year Czech paper rose one basis point on the day to 3.78%. The Czech Finance Ministry auctioned CZK7.0 billion of 15-year bonds at an average yield of 4.29% and average price of 98.49. Demand for the bonds, at CZK8.2 billion, only slightly surpassed supply. The bond has a 3.75% coupon and is a reopening of an issue initially sold in September 2005. The currently traded paper of the same issue had an average yield late Wednesday of 4.16%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                     3402.15  gain  40.32   up 1.2% \par BUX                      23062.17  loss 110.22   dn 0.5% \par PX-D                      1681.40  gain  10.1    up 0.6% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15  3119.01   gain 19.28    up 0.6% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: A broker notes that "if (the FOMC) statements get a warm reception, the WIG20 index is to hold above the 3400 level."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WIG20 index ended up 1.2% at 3402.15. Brokers attributed the positive finish to a lack of negative news rather than a permanent switch to an upward trend. Trading volumes remained low as the market awaited the close of U.S. markets, the FOMC decision and post-meeting statement. Banking stocks were in focus Wednesday, with PKO ending up 4.7%, and Pekao closing 2.1% higher. Among the losers was TPSA, which ended down 0.9% due to market worries about another potential fee by the market regulator.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: A local trader noted that the Central and Eastern European region is "clearly underperforming" and saw no reason why this should change in the short term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX index ended down 0.5% at 23062.17 as drug shares and MOL dragged the index into the red. Shares were also hit by recent forint strength, a trader added. MOL remained under selling pressure and ended 0.8% lower. Richter and Egis also ended lower, down 3.5% and 4.8%, respectively, after Citigroup lowered earnings-per-share and target price on the stocks, a trader said. OTP ended 0.6% higher amid thin volumes, while Magyar Telekom closed up 0.9%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Traders said that Citigroup's rating upgrade of Zentiva, with a new target price of CZK1,800, is likely to drive the stock and the overall market further up in coming days.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PX index ended up 0.6% at 1681.4 led by Zentiva, which closed the day 1.2% higher at CZK1,490. CEZ ended up 1.9% largely on the confirmation that it is ready to buy back some of its stock held by the Czech government. Zentiva and CEZ accounted for over two thirds of $219 million worth of shares traded Wednesday. Among other stocks, Telefonica O2 CR ended down 0.9%, largely on profit-taking after its recent gains. Komercni Banka ended flat at CZK3,543, while Erste Bank closed 0.5% higher after rebounding from last week's losses. [ 03-22-07 0157ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The European Commission is taking Poland to the EU's top court to prevent the construction of a highway that would run across protected habitat in one of Europe's best preserved primeval forests, officials said Wednesday. Polish authorities have approved plans to construct a 10-mile section of the Via Baltica highway linking the country to Finland through a rare peat bog where eagles, wolves and lynx roam. The road would cut through the forest for 500 yards to bypass a town.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Germany's foreign minister on Wednesday pressed the U.S. to consider the price of pushing through plans to locate missile defense facilities in eastern Europe, while his Russian counterpart urged broader consultations with Moscow. In Warsaw, former Polish Defense Minister Radek Sikorski, now a member of the Senate, also cautioned Washington may have more difficulty than it expects in persuading Poland to give final approval to host 10 interceptor missiles. The other part of the U.S. plan envisions a radar base in the Czech Republic.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: A unit of private equity group RPG Wednesday said it had submitted a formal letter of interest to acquire black coal mine Ruch Silesia in southern Poland from its owner Kompania Weglowa SA. Kompania Weglowa, Poland's largest coal mining holding company by revenue, will sell Ruch Silesia because it has posted losses every year since 1995, the company's spokesman Zbigniew Madej told Dow Jones Newswires.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech brewer Budejovicky Budvar NP Wednesday said it is reviewing a decision by an Italian appeals court that stripped it of its rights to the 'Budweiser' trademark in Italy. "At this moment our lawyers are reviewing the issue and we have no further comment," said Petr Samec, spokesman for Budejovicky Budvar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech President Vaclav Klaus said Wednesday that the biggest threat to freedom and democracy around the globe was from what he called "ambitious environmentalism." Klaus, who disputes evidence that human activity is to blame for climate change, made the remark in a written response to questions from a committee in the U.S. Congress.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The European Commission Wednesday warned Denmark, Finland and Hungary to liberalize their domestic gambling and sports betting markets or defend their laws in Europe's highest court. The commission said the countries' rules "restrict the free movement of sports betting services" and aren't "necessary, proportionate and nondiscriminatory".  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary plans to sell digital television licenses next year on condition that legislation regulating the digital television frequency market is approved by parliament this year, Daniel Pataki, president of the telecommunications market authority, or NHH, said Wednesday. "The politicians will need to decide what to base the sale of the licenses on and which frequencies will be used for public service television," Pataki told the press.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary doesn't plan to call a tender this year for the sale of its fourth third-generation mobile telecommunications license, Daniel Pataki, president of the Hungarian telecommunications authority, NHH, said Wednesday. "If anyone comes along offering a fair price, the Hungarian government and the NHH would be willing to sell it. But it's not worth calling a tender without any interest (from a potential buyer)," Pataki told the press.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Renault's Romanian carmaker Automobile Dacia posted a record EUR107.2 million ($142.5 million) profit in 2006 on strong sales of its Logan model, according to figures published Wednesday. The company sold about 187,000 cars, including 79,000 on foreign markets, a 9.3 percent increase compared to 2005, with the strong sales helping it double its profits compared to 2005. Dacia's turnover reached EUR1.5 billion in 2006, up 29 percent from 2005, state news agency Rompres reported, citing company figures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Prime Minister Calin Popescu Tariceanu on Wednesday took over temporarily the foreign ministry portfolio after a dispute with the president on appointing a new minister. President Traian Basescu blocked the appointment of Tariceanu's nominee for the job, senator Adrian Cioroianu, saying that the lawmaker did not have enough experience.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: A Parliamentary commission Wednesday recommended suspending and impeaching President Traian Basescu for violating the constitution. The commission, which was set up to investigate allegations against Basescu made by three opposition parties, also accused the president of committing crimes and said he might be prosecuted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVENIA: The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expects Slovenia to name a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor soon, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said Wednesday. Slovenia hasn't named a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief since Mitja Gaspari finished his term March 12. This has left a vacancy on the ECB's governing council, which oversees monetary policy matters for all euro-zone countries.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Lauren Parr, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9446; lauren.parr@dowjones.com [ 03-22-07 0158ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070322e33m0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\p