\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Slovak Koruna Setting The Tone  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 06:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1200/1100 Hungary Six-Month Treasury Bond Tender \par  \par 1200/1100 Poland Five-Year Treasury Bond Tender \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8756-09  3.8733-83  +0.06  3.8756  3.8664   +1.12 \par EUR/HUF 246.75-05  246.40-00  +0.14  246.81  246.97   -1.86 \par EUR/CZK 27.810-70  27.815-40  -0.02  27.825  27.835   +1.16 \par EUR/SKK 33.370-10  33.348-98  +0.07  33.370  33.390   -3.12 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: The Slovak koruna's trade against the euro is likely to continue to dictate the direction of the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday, traders say. The koruna saw strong gains Monday following Friday's surprise revaluation against the euro. The koruna then fell sharply Tuesday, amid talk the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovak central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The zloty was slightly weaker against both the euro and the dollar Tuesday, tracking the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  koruna. "It seems like the (\b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  koruna) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was carried out through a London-based bank," a Raiffeisenbank trader said. Late Tuesday, the euro was trading at PLN3.8730, from PLN3.8720 late Monday. The dollar was trading at PLN2.9143, from PLN2.9130.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: The Slovak koruna is likely to remain the focus Wednesday, traders say. Further ahead, most analysts expect Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave interest rates on hold at its rate-setting meeting Monday. "We do not expect the National Bank of Hungary to hike rates further from current levels and we expect it to start cutting around the summer," says Goldman Sachs analyst Istvan Zsoldos. DZ Bank's Illes Toth agrees that the Monetary Policy Council should remain cautious and leave rates unchanged Monday. He adds that if the forint manages to keep below HUF250 to the euro, there could be a case for a rate cut. However, JPMorgan's Nora Szentivanyi says that if the forint remains strong, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates in the second quarter rather than in the second half as had been expected.  \par \par \uc2 The forint weakened against the euro Tuesday, tracking the Slovak koruna. Late Tuesday, the euro was at HUF246.50, from HUF245.93 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: Calyon Bank strategist Nigel Rendell says the outlook for the Slovak koruna is more upbeat than that of its neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Czech koruna, the forint and the zloty. "The bullish case for the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not that convincing. The European Union is happy with what Slovakia is doing, but for the other three, monetary union is four to seven years away. Nothing has changed from a fundamental point of view," he says.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna weakened against the euro Tuesday, in line with the Slovak koruna. Late Tuesday, the euro was trading at CZK27.85, from CZK27.73 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: The finance ministry's tender of five-year bonds at 1100 GMT is likely to be the focus Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish government bonds were a touch firmer Tuesday, with yields lower across the curve in a quiet market. Late Tuesday, the average yield on the two-year bond was at 4.50% from 4.51% late Monday. The five-year yield eased to 4.93% from 4.95%, while the 10-year bond dipped to 5.18% from 5.20%. The activity has switched to forward rate agreements on the Warsaw Interbank Offer Rate, traders said. FRAs rose by between five and six basis points along the curve, "clearly saying the first rate hike is due in April or May," a trader said, noting hawkish statements Tuesday by Polish central bankers as the cause.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: Market talk that the Hungarian government could scrap the forint trading band remains strong, despite the finance ministry's denial, a bond trader says. "This is the central issue of the market. It's an open question how much the ministry comment can cool the speculation. This issue won't disappear in the foreseeable future," he says. The focus Wednesday is on the ministry's tender of six-month bonds at 1100 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds were mixed Tuesday, with yields falling on the short end of the curve and rising a touch on the long end. Late Tuesday, the average yield on the three-year bond dropped to 7.31% from 7.36% late Monday. The yield on the five-year bond was unchanged at 7.15%, while the 10-year bond rose to 7.69% from 7.68%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: The government's auction of 15-year bonds is likely to be the focus Wednesday, says a Komercni Banka trader. The ministry aims to sell CZK7 billion of bonds in the auction, which reopens a previous issue of paper with a coupon of 3.75% and maturity in 2020.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Czech government bonds were little changed Tuesday in thin trade. Yields on the short end of the curve fell slightly, tracking bonds on core international markets, while yields on the long end rose a little. Late Tuesday, the average yield on the benchmark 10-year bond was at 3.77% from 3.76% late Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   STOCKS \par  \par WIG20            3361.83     loss   14.95   dn 0.4% \par BUX             23172.39     loss  118.05   dn 0.5% \par PX-D             1671.30     gain   17.60   up 1.1% \par  \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15 3099.73 loss 16.81  dn 0.5% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Consolidation is likely in the near term, with the WIG20 possibly testing 3400 in the coming sessions if global sentiment remains favorable, a trader says.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WIG20 index ended down 0.4% at 3361.83 Tuesday. Traders said a lack of demand from large institutional investors meant that the index failed to sustain Monday's gains. PGNiG closed 3.0% higher after a positive recommendation from a local brokerage, while most other stocks fell. PKN Orlen ended down 1.5%, PKO BP fell 1.4%, TVN dropped 1.1% and TPSA lost 1.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Stocks are likely to hover early Wednesday ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve interest rate decision, a trader says. "The market is usually frozen before the Fed decision, investors are reluctant to open new positions," he says.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX index ended 0.5% lower at 23172.39 Tuesday in thin trade, with most stocks gaining early in the session, only to give up those gains towards the close. "Everyone was optimistic when the market opened and then we gradually started heading south together with other emerging markets," the trader said. OTP gained 0.3%, MOL lost 1.7%, Magyar Telekom fell 1.5% and Richter rose 0.7%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Traders say the outlook for the PX index Wednesday is mixed, with some stocks likely to be under pressure from profit-taking.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index closed 1.1% higher at 1671.3 Tuesday, led by CEZ, which ended up 2.1% as it rebounded from losses Monday. Zentiva ended up 1.8%, as it continued to attract largely overseas investors following the recent announcement that the company had made a Turkish acquisition. Elsewhere, Telefonica O2 CR gained 1.2%, while Komercni Banka dropped 0.6% on profit-taking on its gains last week. [ 03-21-07 0155ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The Hungarian financial market authority PSZAF launched an investigation into the purchase of a combined 42.3% stake in Hungarian chemicals company Tiszai Vegyi Kombinat Nyrt. (TVK.BU) by Hungarian oil and gas company MOL Nyrt. (MOL.BU), a PSZAF official told Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday. "The transaction itself is rather unusual," PSZAF spokesman Istvan Binder said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVENIA: Slovenia, the wealthiest eastern European economy, will probably beat its economic growth goal this year, but fears of overheating are misplaced as the country has undergone a revolution since deciding to join Europe's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  union, Finance Minister Andrej Bajuk has told Dow Jones Newswires.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KOSOVO, SERBIA: Kosovo authorities put up for sale Tuesday a meat processing plant, a dozen shops, a poultry farm and patches of agricultural land in an effort to revive growth and create jobs in the economically depressed province. The Kosovo Trust Agency, the U.N. entity responsible for privatizing the province's companies, said it hoped 32 new firms would be created by the sales, according to a statement. The offered firms also were listed on the agency's Web site. The deadline for submitting bids is May 23.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Susannah Rodgers, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-208-789-8467; susannah.rodgers@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -0 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-21-07 0155ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070321e33l0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\pic