\par\par \b \uc2 Slovak Koruna Revaluation Points To New ERM Flexibility  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leos Rousek  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 15:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE (Dow Jones)--Slovakia remains on course to join the euro zone in 2009 after it secured the agreement of the European Union to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 And Brussels' assent to the 8.5% revaluation of the Slovak koruna should set a precedent for other countries that aim to join the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area early in the next decade, giving them more flexibility to meet one of the key criteria for adopting the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Slovakian authorities, the European Commission and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Friday agreed that the Slovak koruna should trade in a new band with the E.U.'s Exchange Rate Mechanism II. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the koruna from firming above SKK30.1260 against the euro, or weakening below SKK40.7588 to the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Countries that wish to join the euro zone must spend at least two years in the ERM, keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a 15% range above or below a central exchange rate against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia first joined the ERM II in November 2005, when the midpoint of the koruna's trading range was set at SKK38.4550. But with its economy growing at rates closer to those of China than the rest of the E.U., and foreign direct investment flooding into the country's auto and electronics industries, the koruna has appreciated steadily since then.  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  National Bank has prevented the koruna from becoming too strong against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "(The revaluation) is the story of economic convergence," said Juliet Sampson, an economist at HSBC in London. "And the economic convergence is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supportive."  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia isn't alone among candidates to join the euro zone in seeing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years. But the koruna has appreciated more rapidly than the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other countries that joined the European Union in 2004. While the Slovak koruna has appreciated by 10% since late 2005, the Czech koruna has strengthened by 3.8%, and the Polish zloty and Hungarian forint have each appreciated by 2.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia appears likely to enter the euro zone in 2009, some three years before either the Czech Republic, Hungary or Poland join the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area.  \par \par \uc2 It is alone among that group in being realistically able to target a reduction in its budget deficit to less than 3% of gross domestic product by 2009, while it has recently brought its annual rate of inflation down below the maximum allowed under the Maastricht Criteria.  \par \par \uc2 The revaluation of the koruna should help keep the inflation below that maximum level, since a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  means that prices of imported goods should fall, or rise less sharply than would otherwise have been the case.  \par \par \uc2 It is the first time in the history of the ERM II that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been revalued, and may set a precedent for other countries facing a similar dilemma as they near euro-zone membership. But the fact that Slovakia was seeking a revaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than a competitiveness- boosting devaluation, makes it much less surprising that it was granted the additional flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 "They're bending the Maastricht rules and this may imply that the Commission may be a little more lenient on others too in the future," said Nigel Rendell, an economist at Calyon investment bank.  \par \par \uc2 The firming of the Slovak koruna has been driven by the country's stellar economic performance, with gross domestic product growing by 8.3% in 2006 and 6% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The Czech economy grew 6.1% on the year in both 2006 and 2005. Hungary's GDP expanded by 3.9% in 2006 and 4.2% in 2005, while the Polish economy grew 5.8% last year and 3.5% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the Slovak koruna to continue firming in the near-term, although not excessively.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt that this is likely to lead to a strengthening of the (koruna) in the near-term," Lars Christensen, the senior analyst at Danske Bank, wrote in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 At 1400 GMT, the koruna was trading at SKK32.89 against the euro, having weakened from SKK32.02 earlier in the day but strengthened from SKK33.80 late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The revaluation raises new questions about the exact rate at which the koruna is swapped for the euro when the country enters the euro zone in 2009.  \par \par \uc2 The entry rules give ERM members a choice between using the central parity rate or the market spot rate at the time they join the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area.  \par \par \uc2 The Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have so far indicated that the conversion rate would reflect the market rate.  \par \par \uc2 "They are (Slovakia) under no obligation to join at the market rate," Sampson said.  \par \par \uc2 Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 :  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.nbs.sk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.nbs.sk }}}\par \par \uc2 -By Leos Rousek; Dow Jones Newswires; +420 221 085 273; leos.rousek@dowjones.com [ 19-03-07 1420GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070319e33j000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
