\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Slovak cbank sells crowns to halt its fast rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Laca  \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2006\par 10:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds crown close, new analyst quote on crown outlook)  \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, Dec 28 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to halt the crown's record-breaking rise, selling the local unit in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time this year.  \par \par \uc2 The bank said it acted to stop what it saw as an exaggerated move in a thin market.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market; the reason was too quick strengthening of the crown on a market with low liquidity," NBS spokesman Igor Barat said. He gave no further details.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in after the crown hit another all-time high at 34.050 per euro and began selling the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 34.200 to the euro. It sold the crown in several waves pushing it to 34.640 at the close of local trading hours as of 1530 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The crown has risen by more than 10 percent this year, outperforming the rest of central Europe, helped by improved emerging market sentiment and Slovakia's buoyant economic growth which reached almost 10 percent in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "Slovakia is a strong growth story, there is improvement in the balance of payments in prospect and a lot of the concerns over the new government and possible worsening of fiscal policy have not materialised," said Gillian Edgeworth, central Europe economist with Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said they saw more crown firming at least in the first weeks of 2007, unless a major change in investors' mood turned the flow of capital away from emerging markets as such.  \par \par \uc2 "Domestic fundamentals are clearly positive," said Tatra Banka analyst Robert Prega. "The NBS will be in a difficult position if external factors don't work against the crown's firming trend, as the firming pressure is likely to continue."  \par \par \uc2 CLOSE TO LIMIT  \par \par \uc2 This week's gains pushed the crown to 11.5 percent above the crown's central parity and close to the 15 percent upper limit of the ERM-2 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism, which Slovakia joined a year ago as part of its plan to adopt the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2009.  \par \par \uc2 The move sparked market speculation Bratislava may need to revalue the ERM-2 central parity to avoid breaking through the grid's upper limit.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, NBS head Ivan Sramko played down such a scenario, saying it remained a theoretical option in case there was a risk of a lasting break through the ERM trading band.  \par \par \uc2 The crown's strength has helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb inflation, seen as the biggest challenge for the euro adoption plan. But Sramko has suggested some of the recent gains could be reversed because they no longer reflected economic fundamentals and were amplified by low year-end liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have bought 200-250 million euros in Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , much less than the some 3 billion euros that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold in the market to stop the fall of the crown after June elections.  \par \par \uc2 Market sentiment turned after new Prime Minister Robert Fico convinced investors his leftist cabinet remained commited to his centre-right predecessors' euro adoption timetable.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the strong crown had tightened monetary conditions enough to suggest the NBS may be done with raising interest rates, but it was premature to consider an easing.  \par \par \uc2 The bank has raised its main rate by 175 basis points in four steps to 4.75 percent in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "The move (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) might support expectations of a rate cut," said Slovenska Sporitelna analyst Michal Musak.  \par \par \uc2 "But we would be cautious about this, as one of the prime aims of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to meet the inflation criterion, so we think rate cuts should only come once it is sure of meeting it." (Additional reporting by Patrick Graham in Warsaw)  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAKIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|CZS|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061228e2cs000he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
