\par\par \b \uc2 Slovak PM Fico Reiterates Govt Plan To Adopt Euro In '09  \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2006\par 11:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE (Dow Jones)--Slovakia's new prime minister Robert Fico on Saturday said that his government is determined to enter the European Monetary Union in 2009, reiterating his statement from earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "It is in our utmost interest to adopt the euro as of Jan. 1, 2009," Fico said at a conference after meeting his Czech counterpart Jiri Paroubek.  \par \par \uc2 "The new Slovak government will do all what is necessary so that we meet all criteria required for the euro adoption in 2009," Fico added.  \par \par \uc2 Fico's left-of-center SMER-Social Democratic Party, or SMER, won the June 17 poll by attracting disgruntled voters who failed to reap fruits from the former Communist country's transition to a market economy. Fico has promised to undo some of the belt-tightening measures, such as health care payments and welfare cuts, introduced by the former right-of-center government.  \par \par \uc2 This has prompted concerns among investors that Fico's government will fail to make Slovakia fiscally fit to switch to the euro. [ 15-07-06 0958GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times since mid-June to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak\b0 \highlight\uc2  koruna to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-offs prompted by investors' fears that adoption of the euro will be delayed because of rising fiscal spending and spiraling inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, seeking to placate market jitters, Fico also said that his government will coordinate its economic policies with the Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will honor the euro-adoption criteria, including a budget deficit below 3% of gross domestic product, as spelled out in the 1992 Maastricht Treaty on the European Monetary Union.  \par \par \uc2 While winning most votes in the June polls, SMER didn't get an outright majority in the 150-seat Slovak parliament.  \par \par \uc2 Fico has stunned most local market participants by forming his coalition government with two fringe parties: the Slovak Nationalists, or SNS, and populist People's Party Movement for Democratic Slovakia, or LS-HZDS.  \par \par \uc2 Fico's decision to team up with SNS and LS-HZDS has raised criticism of some politicians in the European Union's parliament.  \par \par \uc2 After meeting Fico Saturday on his first state visit abroad after taking over the Slovak government, the Czech Prime Minister sought to calm down such concerns, saying that the new Slovak government should be judged by its deeds and not impressions.  \par \par \uc2 -By Leos Rousek, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 221 085 273; leos.rousek@dowjones.com [ 15-07-06 1006GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060715e27f00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
