\par\par \b \uc2 Slovakia - Factors To Watch on July 13  \b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2006\par 08:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRATISLAVA, July 13 (Reuters) - Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch which may affect Slovak financial markets on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 PM, FINMIN TO MEET CBANK GOV ON EURO ADOPTION  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Robert Fico and Finance Minister Jan Pociatek will meet with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Ivan Sramko to debate Slovakia's euro adoption plans (1300 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 PM ROBERT FICO TO MEET REGIONAL CHIEFS  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Robert Fico will meet chiefs of all eight regions to discuss their future cooperation (1000 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 CBANK DEFENDS CROWN, MARKET STAYS EDGY  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovak central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday to defend the crown as an edgy mood on emerging markets and a shaky domestic political outlook pushed investors to dump more Slovak assets.  \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL12750626]  \par \par \uc2 CPI BELOW FCAST BUT RATE RISE STILL SEEN  \par \par \uc2 Slovak consumer prices grew slightly less than expected in June but stubbornly high inflation is still likely to prompt a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening of policy later this month.  \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL12434329]  \par \par \uc2 JUNE CPI SLIGHTLY BELOW FCAST  \par \par \uc2 Slovak consumer prices rose by 0.1 percent month-on-month in June, putting the annual inflation rate at 4.6 percent, data released by the Statistics Office showed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 [ID:nL12408147]  \par \par \uc2 MAY FOREIGN TRADE GAP SKK 7.8 BLN  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia's foreign trade balance showed a deficit of 7.8 billion crowns ($258.5 million) in May, shrinking from a revised gap of 9.9 billion crowns in April, the Statistics Office said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 [ID:nPRG000019]  \par \par \uc2 HEATING PRICES SEEN UP - PRODUCERS  \par \par \uc2 The daily Sme reports on Thursday that the Slovak Association of Heat Producers said it could ask the state regulator to increase heating prices for households by 3 percent in September, or early in 2007 due to the higher cost of gas. The regulator approved a 10 to 13 percent hike in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY PRICES FOR HOUSEHOLDS SEEN UP 5 PCT IN 2007  \par \par \uc2 Slovak energy utility Slovenske Elektrarne (SE), controlled by Italy's Enel, said will raise energy prices by 16 percent effective from January 2007, which will raise energy prices for households by around 5 percent, the daily Pravda reported.  \par \par \uc2 SMER VICE-CHAIRMAN JOLTS PARTY MAINSTREAM  \par \par \uc2 Smer's Vice-Chairman Monika Benova, once a close ally of Prime Minister Robert Fico, criticises Fico for a coalition with the far-right Nationalist and Vladimir Meciar's HZDS adding Fico's anti-eurosocialist rhetoric not appropriate, the daily Sme reports.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAKS EYE FOUR BIDS IN MOBILE LICENCE TENDER  \par \par \uc2 Slovakia, which launched an international tender for a third mobile phone operator in May, is expected to disclose the names of all interested bidders on Friday. Local madia have reported Austria's Telekom, Spanish Telefonica, British Vodafone and Slovak private equities groups Penta and Slavia Capital were among the expected bidders.  \par \par \uc2 For an economic indicator diary for the euro zone, the United States and other Group of Seven countries, see <G7TODAY>. For a diary of forthcoming Slovak events, double click [SK/DIARY], and for a calendar of east European economic indicators, see [CONV/DIARY].  \par \par \uc2 News editor of the day: Peter Laca on +421 2 5341 8402; fax: +421 2 5341 8403  \par \par \uc2 E-mail: reuters@editorial.sk  \par \par \uc2 Reuters Messaging: peter.laca.com@reuters.net For real-time index quo